\description{
\strong{Field-goal attempts of one B.LEAGUE game with their court position (play-by-play
rows that carry x / y).}

Endpoint: \verb{GET https://b-league.s3.amazonaws.com/web_json/v2_genius_contexts/\{schedule_key\}/\{latest\}.json}
(the v1 feed, or the game page's embed, when v2 does not hold the game).

One row per field-goal attempt (\code{ActionCD1} 1-6) with a location, oldest first. \code{x}
is the percent (0-100) of the court LENGTH from the \code{left} baseline and \code{y} the
percent of the WIDTH (the baskets sit near x = 5 and 95 at y = 50). Which sideline is
y = 0 is unverified, and metres (x / 100 x 28, y / 100 x 15) are an inference. \code{side}
(\code{left} / \code{right}) names the basket a shot is charted at, from 2025-26 only; teams
change ends at half-time. \code{in_paint} is \code{NA} on the B3 feed. Locations exist from
2022-23 on the v2 feed only: any other game gives a zero-row tibble.
}
\details{
The B.LEAGUE (Japan Professional Basketball League: B1, B2, B3) wrappers mirror
sdv-py's \code{sportsdataverse.basketball.bleague} wrappers of the same names: same
arguments, the same snake_case columns and types, one row per sdv-py row.

\strong{Unofficial and unsupported.} The game contexts are JSON objects in
B.LEAGUE's own public S3 bucket (\code{b-league.s3.amazonaws.com}, the store its game
pages read), and the schedule, standings and game_detail pages of
\code{www.bleague.jp} are parsed here. Neither host publishes terms for this use
(the site's /terms/ answers 404; robots.txt sets a Crawl-delay only for msnbot
and bingbot). Requests are spaced at least one second apart per host; 429 and
5xx answers are retried (three attempts). SportsDataverse has not ruled on the
site's terms, so the data is not redistributed as release assets.

\strong{Seasons} are END years (\code{season = 2026} is 2025-26); the site's own \code{Year} /
\verb{year=} is the START year, so the request sends \code{year = season - 1}. Game
wrappers take the global \code{schedule_key} (one integer sequence across B1, B2, B3
and every season: 1 = the 2016-17 opener, 5856 in 2020-21, 500017 in 2022-23,
504734 / 505508 B1 / B2 and 10693 B3 in 2025-26); list a day's keys with
\code{\link[=bleague_schedule]{bleague_schedule()}}.

\strong{Feeds.} A game is read from the v2 feed (B1/B2 from 2020-21), else the v1
feed (B3 and B1/B2 2018-2020), else the game page's embedded copy (the only
source of 2016-17 and 2017-18), decided at run time. v2 ids are strings, v1 ids
integers: both become text here. v1 adds leaders and the score track; the
team-rebound codes 18 / 19 are swapped between the feeds and a v1 substitution
is one row where v2 has two. Player and team names are Japanese (English names
only on the box score and header). Game wrappers serve FINISHED games only.
Each game wrapper fetches the context itself (two requests): fetch once per
game per wrapper you need.

\strong{Errors} are classed conditions, never a warning plus an empty frame:
\code{hoopR_no_data} (no source holds the game, a schedule day without games, HTTP
404), \code{hoopR_bad_request} (a non-integer or out-of-range argument, HTTP 400 /
422) and \code{hoopR_asset_fetch} (any other failure: another status, a transport
error, a non-JSON or non-HTML body, an error or changed body, an unfinished
game, a context or page for another game, season, tab or day, a truncated
schedule list, or layout drift). All inherit \code{hoopR_error}.
}
